\begin{abstract}
Reliable tag extraction from piping and instrumentation diagrams can support
document indexing, engineering-data migration, and candidate retrieval, but a
high vision--language model score does not by itself establish that the model
read the correct drawing. We present SABER-PID, a source-isolated
counterfactual evaluation workflow that separates image evidence from
same-drawing retrieval and language priors while recording actual input and
output budgets. On 100 source-disjoint PIDQA drawings, frozen Qwen3-VL-8B at a
3072-side, 192-token operating point achieved tag precision @@QWEN_P@@, recall
@@QWEN_R@@, F1 @@QWEN_F1@@, and exact-set accuracy @@QWEN_EXACT@@. Correct
images exceeded source-shuffled and no-image controls by @@E3@@ and @@E8@@ F1,
respectively. The 3072-minus-768 effect remained @@E2@@ at a 512-token cap and
@@E6_29@@/@@E6_31@@ across two descriptive source partitions. Frozen
full-image PaddleOCR reached F1 @@OCR_F1@@. A reference-free descriptive
fusion analysis exposed complementary operating modes: set union raised
recall to @@UNION_R@@ and F1 to @@UNION_F1@@, whereas set intersection reached
precision @@INTERSECTION_P@@. @@FUSION_VALIDATION_ABSTRACT@@ Source isolation reduced an
input-retrieval-plus-prior diagnostic from @@R1_RANDOM@@ to @@R1_SOURCE@@,
showing why drawing-level separation matters. These results establish a
reproducible route to image-grounded candidate-tag retrieval and configurable
precision--recall trade-offs. They do not establish general topology
reasoning, and PIDQA remains a single synthetic source family.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} piping and instrumentation diagrams; vision--language
models; engineering document intelligence; tag extraction; source isolation;
counterfactual evaluation; multimodal information retrieval

\section{Introduction}

Piping and instrumentation diagrams encode equipment, instruments, process
lines, and control relationships in visually dense engineering documents.
Their tags connect drawings to equipment lists, maintenance records, and
engineering databases. Reliable tag retrieval is therefore a practical first
step toward document indexing and data migration, particularly when legacy
drawings must be searched before complete graph reconstruction is available.

Modern vision--language models offer a flexible interface for this task, but
their apparent competence is difficult to interpret. One drawing generates
many related questions, small tags compete with long-range topology, and a
model can answer from task regularities without using the requested image. A
useful engineering result must therefore show not only that a configuration
scores well, but that its gain follows the correctly paired drawing and
persists under explicit operating controls.

Most P\&ID research has therefore focused on symbol detection, association,
graph reconstruction, and end-to-end digitization \cite{rahul2019,paliwal2021,
stuermer2025,prasad2026}. PIDQA adds 64,000 graph-derived questions over 500
synthetic source sheets \cite{gupta2025}. This creates a useful stress test for
multimodal question answering, but it also creates a measurement problem:
questions from the same drawing are statistically and visually dependent.
Random question splitting can expose a route unavailable on an unseen
drawing, a special case of the leakage and grouping failures documented more
broadly in machine-learning evaluation \cite{kapoor2023,geras2013}.

Document and chart VQA benchmarks demonstrate that text recognition and
layout reasoning interact \cite{mathew2021,masry2022}. Large VLM evaluation
also shows that fluent outputs need not be visually supported
\cite{li2023pope,leng2024}. A P\&ID evaluation should therefore answer a more
operational question than ``which configuration is larger?'': \emph{which
document capability is reliably available, and which evidence supports it?}

We address that question with \emph{SABER-PID}: \textbf{S}ource-isolated,
\textbf{A}nswer-hidden, \textbf{B}udget-recorded,
\textbf{E}vidence-counterfactual, and \textbf{R}eported-by-task evaluation.
It is a reproducible engineering measurement workflow rather than a model
architecture. The contribution is threefold:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em,itemsep=2pt]
\item drawing-level source isolation and answer-hidden inference that remove
an available same-source route from the primary evaluation;
\item correct-image, source-shuffled, and no-image interventions that localize
a large high-detail Qwen tag-reading effect across output caps and source
partitions; and
\item a truth-free OCR--VLM fusion analysis that converts complementary errors
into configurable coverage, precision, and balanced tag-retrieval modes.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related work and evaluation objective}

Digitize-PID released a synthetic P\&ID resource and an end-to-end
digitization pipeline \cite{paliwal2021}; PIDQA paired those drawings with
executable graph-query questions across connectivity, count, spatial-count,
and value tasks \cite{gupta2025}. Recent P\&ID studies explore graph-oriented
and multimodal workflows \cite{alimin2025,zhu2026}. Engineering-diagram
benchmarks such as Enginuity broaden this multimodal setting
\cite{kumar2026}, while DocVQA and ChartQA show the distinct roles of visual
text and structure in document question answering \cite{mathew2021,masry2022}.

The present work is complementary. We do not train a new architecture or
claim leaderboard leadership. We use frozen Qwen3-VL and InternVL checkpoints
\cite{bai2025,wang2025}, a specialized OCR comparator
\cite{du2020}, and controlled input interventions to identify what an observed
score can support. This follows a measurement view of VLM reliability: image
removal and image corruption expose dependence on visual evidence, while
source grouping and hidden references protect the evaluation boundary.

\section{Materials and methods}

\subsection{Dataset, source identity, and frozen evaluation set}

PIDQA contains 64,000 questions from 500 Dataset-PID sheets, with 16,000
questions in each of four task families. We retain the source sheet as the
clustering and splitting unit. The primary Set B evaluation contains one
deterministically selected record per source and task for 100 source sheets
(400 records). Public identifiers are ranked by SHA-256; answers, Cypher
queries, and model outputs do not enter selection. Every model-facing manifest
contains the instance ID, source ID, task, question, image path, and public
template fields only. References remain in a scorer-only store.

The task-prior comparator uses only the seed-17 source-isolated training
partition. It predicts the most frequent normalized training answer within
each task and receives no image. The final answer-isolation audit covers
@@AUDIT_INPUTS@@ public manifests and @@AUDIT_OUTPUTS@@ raw output files from
the primary E2--E8 controls; no public input contains \texttt{answer} or
\texttt{cypher}.

\subsection{Input-retrieval-plus-prior split diagnostic}

For seeds 3, 17, 29, 43, and 71, question-random and source-isolated splits use
60\% training, 20\% calibration, and 20\% test allocations. The reported L5
diagnostic builds an unambiguous cache over training records keyed by the
exact source-image SHA-256 and a frozen semantic signature of the released
question template and public fields. A hit returns the unique cached training
answer; a miss falls back to the training-only task majority. The diagnostic
uses neither learned embeddings nor nearest neighbours, and \texttt{source\_id}
is not a cache key. Each split has 38,400 training and 12,800 test questions.
Image plus semantic cache coverage is present only when the identical drawing
appears across the random split. This is an input-recoverable route audit, not
an observation of VLM behaviour.

\subsection{Frozen models, prompt, and counterfactual controls}

The primary instrument is frozen Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct with greedy decoding and
the pre-specified P0 prompt. Qwen receives a maximum image side of 768 or 3072
and an output cap of 192 or 512, as labelled for each contrast. E2 changes the
output cap to 512 on the 100 value records. E3 replaces each P\&ID with a
deterministic Sattolo source derangement (100 sources, no fixed point). E8
retains model, prompt, decoder, question, and 192-token cap while removing all
image content. E5 adds a fixed public symbol-prototype legend as a second
image; E7 preserves its crops, grid, font, dimensions, and image count while
cyclically shifting numeric labels 1--32. E6 retains two pre-specified source
partitions as descriptive sensitivity analyses.

The cross-family extension uses frozen InternVL3.5-8B on the same 100 value
records with correct, source-shuffled, and no-image conditions, seven realised
448-pixel tiles for image conditions, a 512-token cap, and the tokenizer's
documented Mistral-regex correction enabled. The OCR comparator
uses frozen PaddleOCR 2.8.1 / PaddlePaddle 2.6.2 with the English pipeline
(PP-OCRv3 detector and PP-OCRv4 recognizer) on each complete drawing, no crop,
no angle classifier, and detector maximum side 3072. Candidate tags are parsed
by the same legal grammar and filtered only by
the question's public prefix; no answer or test-set tuning is available.

\subsection{Reference-free OCR--VLM fusion modes}

The frozen Qwen and geometry-joined OCR tag sets are combined without scores,
references, or learned parameters. Four simple rules are retained: set union,
set intersection, OCR if non-empty with Qwen fallback, and Qwen if non-empty
with OCR fallback. Union targets candidate coverage; intersection targets a
high-precision shortlist; and the fallback rules retain one instrument's
output when it is available. The complete rule family is reported. Because
these combinations were analysed after the individual Qwen and OCR results,
the fusion study is explicitly descriptive and is not presented as an
independently validated model-selection result.

\subsection{Scoring, operating quantities, and uncertainty}

Strict scoring normalizes Booleans, one integer, or a set of comma-separated
tags. A separately reported conservative semantic parser accepts a leading
Boolean, a single integer token, or a frozen tag grammar; it never consults the
reference. Value questions report tag precision, recall, F1, and exact-set
accuracy together. Empty truth and prediction sets receive exact agreement;
duplicates are removed before set scoring. All paired intervals use 10,000
source-cluster bootstrap replicates with \texttt{source\_id} as the resampling
unit and comparison-specific fixed seeds. No post-hoc equivalence margin is
introduced.

Recorded operating quantities include processor tensor elements or realised
tiles, output cap, mean and 95th-percentile generated tokens, cap rate,
end-to-end generation latency, and peak allocated GPU memory where available.
Latency is not interpreted as visual-encoding cost because generation length
co-varies with the condition.

