\subsection{Source isolation improves the validity of the reported gain}

Across five fixed split seeds, the input-retrieval-plus-prior diagnostic
scores @@R1_RANDOM@@ under question-random splitting and @@R1_SOURCE@@ under
source isolation, a mean gap of @@R1_GAP@@ percentage points. Individual gaps
range from @@R1_GAP_MIN@@ to @@R1_GAP_MAX@@ points. The additional route comes
from exact-image semantic cache hits and is therefore available only when the
same drawing crosses the split. This diagnostic does not assert that the
evaluated VLM exploited that route; it establishes why the headline
tag-reading result is reported on unseen source drawings.

\subsection{Boundary analyses delimit the application claim}

The positive result is specific to tag retrieval. At the same 3072/192 point,
aggregate strict accuracy is @@CORRECT_ACC@@ with the correct image and
@@TEXT_ACC@@ without an image because structural tasks retain strong prior
pathways; the full task matrix is reported in Supplementary Figure S1. A
visible legend changes spatial-count accuracy, but correct numeric labels show
no detected advantage over a layout-matched permutation (95\% interval,
@@E7_SHORT_CI@@). The tokenizer-corrected InternVL3.5-8B extension produces
correct-image F1 @@IV_CORRECT_F1@@ versus zero with wrong/no image, a small
detected direction rather than a practical replication. These results remain
in the supplement and prevent the tag-retrieval result from being generalized
to topology reasoning, arbitrary model families, or a universal detail law.

\section{Discussion}

The principal engineering result is that high-detail tag retrieval can be
validated as an image-grounded capability rather than inferred from an
aggregate benchmark score. The effect remains large under two output caps,
two source partitions, a source-shuffled drawing, and complete image removal.
This combination is stronger than a resolution comparison alone: it links the
gain to the requested source drawing and defines the operating point at which
the capability is available.

The OCR--VLM analysis turns that evidence into a practical retrieval design.
For broad candidate generation, set union increases recall to @@UNION_R@@ and
F1 to @@UNION_F1@@. For a conservative shortlist, set intersection reaches
precision @@INTERSECTION_P@@. The OCR-first fallback provides a balanced mode
with F1 @@OCR_FALLBACK_F1@@ and exact-set accuracy @@OCR_FALLBACK_EXACT@@.
These modes are transparent set operations rather than a new trained model,
so their outputs can be inspected, indexed, and checked by deterministic
downstream rules. The initial Set B comparison was post-hoc; the complete rule
family was subsequently frozen before the seed-29/31 checks. Evaluation on a
second drawing family remains the next external-validity target.

SABER-PID contributes the evidence discipline needed to interpret these
capabilities. Drawing-level splits remove an available same-source route;
answer-hidden manifests separate inference from scoring; actual budgets make
conditions comparable; and wrong/no-image interventions test source identity
and visual presence directly. This workflow is applicable to engineering
document intelligence whenever many queries share one source artifact.

The boundary controls do not overturn the positive application result; they
locate it. Structural tasks, numeric legend semantics, and the second frozen
model do not show the same practical magnitude. They are therefore retained
in the supplement and summarized in the main text, while the paper's central
claim remains the capability directly supported by convergent controls:
source-specific candidate-tag retrieval from high-detail drawings.

\section{Limitations}

PIDQA is a single public synthetic 500-sheet source family. Source isolation
protects unseen-sheet evaluation within that resource but does not establish
performance on proprietary, scanned, or revision-marked plant drawings. The
primary positive result concerns one frozen Qwen3-VL-8B prompt and decoding
configuration; the much smaller InternVL3.5-8B effect shows that magnitude is
not model-invariant. The OCR comparator is a frozen full-image pipeline rather
than an optimized industrial recognizer. The initial Set B fusion comparison
is post-hoc; frozen-rule seed checks remain within the same family and do not
replace prospective validation on a second source family.

The 100-source controls estimate one benchmark operating point rather than a
universal detail law. Seed 29 and seed 31 are descriptive partitions, not
independent datasets, and latency co-varies with generated length. The
external PID2Graph/OPEN100 branch remains unavailable because the observed
archive failed official size, MD5, and ZIP-central-directory checks; no member
was extracted and no external score is reported.

\section{Conclusion}

High-detail vision--language processing provides a substantial,
source-specific tag-retrieval signal on source-isolated PIDQA drawings.
Correct/wrong/no-image controls establish that the gain follows the requested
drawing, while output-cap and source-partition analyses show a stable positive
direction. OCR and Qwen errors are complementary: reference-free union reaches
recall @@UNION_R@@ and F1 @@UNION_F1@@, while intersection reaches precision
@@INTERSECTION_P@@. Frozen-rule source-partition checks quantify the stability
of the union mode without re-selection. SABER-PID converts these observations into a reproducible
engineering evaluation workflow with explicit evidence boundaries. The
supported application is candidate-tag retrieval and document intelligence;
prospective validation on a second drawing family is the next step toward
field deployment.

\section*{Data and code availability}

The accompanying reproducibility materials contain answer-isolated public
manifests, scorer-only references, immutable raw generation JSONL,
deterministic scorers, source-cluster reports, figure generators, and SHA-256
release manifests. PIDQA/Dataset-PID source data are CC0 in the vendored
license record; acquisition-by-reference remains the default for full image
collections. Model weights are not redistributed. The archival DOI or public
repository URL is submitter-owned and must be inserted before upload:
\texttt{[SUBMITTER: ARCHIVE DOI/URL]}.

